  "worked_solution": "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f(\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡y)={2}^{2\\cdot (1/2{log}_{2}⁡y)}=y.$\n\n- II is an into function. Let's take $\\pi \\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $g(x)=|x|=\\pi .$ The only numbers $x$ that satisfy $g(x)=\\pi $ are $x=\\pm \\pi \\notin Q.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={x}^{2}=-1.$ In fact, ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
